  <content>
  <section><title>Using Graphs to Find Values</title>
<para>Two functions, \(f(x)\) and \(g(x)\), are graphed below.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/986c876b6a7ce62300a07a46d57b9ba670fc452d"/></media></figure></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">What is the value of \(f(-1)\)?</para>
<para>Look at where \(x=-1\) touches the graph of \(f\). The \(y\)-value is –3.</para>
<para>\(f(-1)=-3\)</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">What is the value of \(g(-1)\)?</para>
<para>Look where \(x=-1\) touches the graph of \(g\). The \(y\)-value is –1.</para>
<para>\(g(-1)=-1\)</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Which value is greater?</para>
<para>\(g(-1)&gt;f(-1)\)</para>
<title>Try It: Using Graphs to Find Values</title>
<para>Two functions, \(f(x)\) and \(g(x)\), are graphed below.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/0a95f97942851a42c0c3da2b29964995d4775d74"/></media></figure></para>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Which is greater, \(f(1)\) or \(g(1)\)?</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
    </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to determine which value is greater:</para>
  <para>Looking at the graph, \(f(1)\) is where \(x=1\) reaches the graph of \(f(x)\). The \(y\)-value there is 4. So, \(f(1)=4\).</para>
  <para>Similarly, \(g(1)\) is where \(x=1\) reaches the graph of \(g(x)\). The \(y\)-value there is 2.</para>
  <para>So, \(f(1) &gt;g(1)\).</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
